    },
    "body": "This improves our `Sock` test coverage following CVE-2026-117350.\n\nThis leaves out the test for the specific case that led to the OOM until we [fix `select()` reporting](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36688#issue-6085527087) for urgent data, but i'm also trying not to fight the last battle too much. Instead i focused on trying to pin down all invariants Claude and I could come up with that could be reasonably tested. This will help us understand our code better, and get more assurance we do not break some assumption a (security critical) caller may be relying on further down the road.\n\nAnother goal is to tie the events our fuzzing mock can report to the events each OS actually reports, and when. A follow-up will extend the mock to match what these tests pin down. This wouldn’t have caught the CVE-2026-117350 bug: it was in the very logic the mock replaces, so a fuzz target could only have found it if the mock had been written with that bug in mind. But the next bug may well be somewhere else, and a mock that can report everything the real socket APIs can, lets the fuzzer reach corner cases which may lack a proper unit test.\n\nA lot more coverage could be added. I chose not to add tests specific to the logic used for trusted interfaces (Tor control, the SOCKS5 proxy, I2P SAM) or to a single platform. I also left out the test which led me to find https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36688#issuecomment-6509375262, as this needs to be fixed first.",
