      "message": "qa: pin down the socket readiness that callers of Sock::Wait() rely on\n\nTest what Sock::Wait() and Sock::WaitMany() report, under a matrix of\ndifferent conditions about the state of the socket and the requested\nevents.\n\nA few notable invariants exercised here:\n- Only what's requested is reported (in particular, no unrequested\n  RecvEvent when we took care not to ask for it, see CVE-2026-117350).\n- Nothing readable means no RecvEvent (an idle connection never wakes a\n  wait for receiving).\n- A reset wakes a wait for SendEvent, and the send() fails for good.\n- A reported event always means we can make progress (avoid spins).\n- Wait() and WaitMany() are equivalent.\n\nReadiness is level-triggered, so the tests first make sure that what the\npeer did has arrived and then check what a wait reports. They do not\nrely on anything not happening within some time, which would be brittle.\n\nWhere the select() and poll() backends differ, the difference is spelled\nout in one place ahead of the tests, so that a change of backend has to\nupdate it. Callers cope with these differences, except with known\nquirks. For instance, Windows' select() fails when no socket requests\nanything.",
      "committer": {
        "name": "Antoine Poinsot",
        "email": "mail@antoinep.com",
        "date": "2026-11-02T22:22:34Z"
      },
      "author": {
